  "sourceUrl": "https://www.fs.com/uk/products/157694.html",
  "notes": "The port census is the product name and the page repeats it: '**48 x 10G SFP+ with 8 x 100G QSFP28 Ports**'. So 48 SFP+ cages that take 1G or 10G optics, and 8 QSFP28 cages that take 40G or 100G - the platform also handles 25G and 1G/40G networks, which is why both cages carry a ladder rather than a single rate. It is an **active** packet broker, not a passive tap: it aggregates, filters, replicates, de-duplicates and load-balances L2-L4 traffic and can rewrite headers and timestamps. So it declares `l2-forward` (the catalogue's active-tap shape, like the GigaVAVE and Keysight brokers) and **no** `passive: true`, and it has a management surface, hence `cli` and `http-server`. A lab inserting it should expect a device that can drop traffic by policy, not a wire that copies it. **Not modelled: a PoE or power figure.** The page publishes neither, and there is nothing here that powers a peer - all 56 ports are cages."
